1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,629
<font color="#FFFFFF">DRAGONN is a randomized hashing algorithm for
gradient sparsification in data-parallel distributed training,</font>

2
00:00:07,709 --> 00:00:09,831
<font color="#FFFFFF">from Rice University and ThirdAI.</font>

3
00:00:09,911 --> 00:00:11,433
<font color="#FFFFFF">In distributed training,</font>

4
00:00:11,513 --> 00:00:16,106
<font color="#FFFFFF">synchronizing gradients across GPUs is the main
efficiency bottleneck,</font>

5
00:00:16,186 --> 00:00:19,777
<font color="#FFFFFF">and gradient sparsification methods were meant to help,</font>

6
00:00:19,857 --> 00:00:24,115
<font color="#FFFFFF">but their own compression overhead has become the
new bottleneck.</font>

7
00:00:24,195 --> 00:00:30,790
<font color="#FFFFFF">DRAGONN replaces the exact parallel-prefix-sum
operations used by prior methods with direct hashing,</font>

8
00:00:30,870 --> 00:00:36,597
<font color="#FFFFFF">cutting compression time by up to seventy percent
and delivering up to three-and-a-half</font>

9
00:00:36,677 --> 00:00:38,800
<font color="#FFFFFF">times faster training throughput.</font>

10
00:00:39,180 --> 00:00:45,387
<font color="#FFFFFF">Data-parallel distributed training is the standard
way to scale deep learning across many GPUs,</font>

11
00:00:45,467 --> 00:00:49,689
<font color="#FFFFFF">but synchronizing gradients between workers is the
dominant cost.</font>

12
00:00:49,769 --> 00:00:55,579
<font color="#FFFFFF">Gradient sparsification promised relief by
transmitting only a small subset of gradients,</font>

13
00:00:55,659 --> 00:01:01,403
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet in practice the time spent compressing the
gradients grew so large that it cancelled</font>

14
00:01:01,483 --> 00:01:03,388
<font color="#FFFFFF">out the communication savings.</font>

15
00:01:03,468 --> 00:01:07,822
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper shows that once a tensor exceeds about
sixteen megabytes,</font>

16
00:01:07,902 --> 00:01:12,786
<font color="#FFFFFF">this compression overhead becomes the single
largest efficiency bottleneck,</font>

17
00:01:12,866 --> 00:01:18,080
<font color="#FFFFFF">and for small tensors sparsification can even be
slower than sending everything.</font>

18
00:01:18,460 --> 00:01:22,557
<font color="#FFFFFF">The leading sparsification method, Deep Gradient
Compression,</font>

19
00:01:22,637 --> 00:01:28,173
<font color="#FFFFFF">selects gradients above an estimated threshold,
which is an approximate operation.</font>

20
00:01:28,253 --> 00:01:34,473
<font color="#FFFFFF">But to place those gradients into memory without
conflicts it relies on parallel prefix sum,</font>

21
00:01:34,553 --> 00:01:38,171
<font color="#FFFFFF">an exact algorithm that builds a balanced binary tree,</font>

22
00:01:38,251 --> 00:01:40,705
<font color="#FFFFFF">runs in logarithmic sequential steps,</font>

23
00:01:40,785 --> 00:01:45,841
<font color="#FFFFFF">and touches memory about seven times more than the
theoretical lower bound.</font>

24
00:01:45,921 --> 00:01:53,510
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors call this an exact-approximate
mismatch: since only an approximate set of top gradients is required,</font>

25
00:01:53,590 --> 00:01:58,030
<font color="#FFFFFF">using an expensive exact algorithm to place them is
wasted effort.</font>

26
00:01:58,410 --> 00:02:01,922
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes three contributions.</font>

27
00:02:02,002 --> 00:02:05,513
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, it proposes DRAGONN,</font>

28
00:02:05,593 --> 00:02:09,105
<font color="#FFFFFF">a hashing-based sparsification algorithm that
slashes compression overhead while keeping</font>

29
00:02:09,185 --> 00:02:12,697
<font color="#FFFFFF">the same per-iteration convergence,</font>

30
00:02:12,777 --> 00:02:16,288
<font color="#FFFFFF">backed by theoretical bounds on both compression
error and generalization error.</font>

31
00:02:16,368 --> 00:02:19,880
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

32
00:02:19,960 --> 00:02:23,472
<font color="#FFFFFF">it adds two system-level optimizations: an
efficiency-aware tensor selection that only</font>

33
00:02:23,552 --> 00:02:27,063
<font color="#FFFFFF">compresses tensors where it actually pays off,</font>

34
00:02:27,143 --> 00:02:30,655
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a sparse decoding scheme that keeps decode cost
from growing with the number of GPUs.</font>

35
00:02:30,735 --> 00:02:34,247
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

36
00:02:34,327 --> 00:02:37,838
<font color="#FFFFFF">it evaluates the method broadly across vision and
recommendation models and demonstrates</font>

37
00:02:37,918 --> 00:02:41,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">substantial end-to-end training speedups.</font>

38
00:02:41,810 --> 00:02:47,571
<font color="#FFFFFF">DRAGONN works by pre-allocating a small memory
buffer sized to the compression ratio.</font>

39
00:02:47,651 --> 00:02:51,695
<font color="#FFFFFF">For each gradient whose absolute value clears the
threshold,</font>

40
00:02:51,775 --> 00:02:56,093
<font color="#FFFFFF">it hashes the gradient's index to a slot and writes
it directly,</font>

41
00:02:56,173 --> 00:02:59,529
<font color="#FFFFFF">rather than scanning to compute nonzero positions.</font>

42
00:02:59,609 --> 00:03:04,202
<font color="#FFFFFF">If two indices collide, the later one simply
overwrites the earlier,</font>

43
00:03:04,282 --> 00:03:06,745
<font color="#FFFFFF">and any slot left empty maps to zero.</font>

44
00:03:06,825 --> 00:03:10,456
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because writing memory is an atomic operation on GPUs,</font>

45
00:03:10,536 --> 00:03:16,023
<font color="#FFFFFF">many threads can hash and write at the same time
with no dependency between them,</font>

46
00:03:16,103 --> 00:03:21,040
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the whole compression needs only d comparisons
plus l hash operations,</font>

47
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:23,788
<font color="#FFFFFF">essentially the theoretical lower bound.</font>

48
00:03:23,868 --> 00:03:25,025
<font color="#FFFFFF">To deploy it well,</font>

49
00:03:25,105 --> 00:03:31,142
<font color="#FFFFFF">DRAGONN adds two tricks: it only compresses a
tensor when the compression time is smaller</font>

50
00:03:31,222 --> 00:03:33,685
<font color="#FFFFFF">than the communication it would save,</font>

51
00:03:33,765 --> 00:03:40,076
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it batches all the compressed tensors received
from other workers into one sparse decode,</font>

52
00:03:40,156 --> 00:03:45,230
<font color="#FFFFFF">so decoding cost stays nearly constant no matter
how many GPUs participate.</font>

53
00:03:45,610 --> 00:03:52,499
<font color="#FFFFFF">The experiments span four models covering both
vision and recommendation-style workloads:</font>

54
00:03:52,579 --> 00:03:54,770
<font color="#FFFFFF">ResNet50 trained on ImageNet,</font>

55
00:03:54,850 --> 00:04:01,426
<font color="#FFFFFF">Vision Transformer and MLP-Mixer fine-tuned on
Cifar10 from ImageNet-21k pretraining,</font>

56
00:04:01,506 --> 00:04:07,221
<font color="#FFFFFF">and an extreme multi-label classification model on
the Wiki10-31K dataset.</font>

57
00:04:07,301 --> 00:04:15,287
<font color="#FFFFFF">The testbed is sixteen Nvidia V100 GPUs across two
machines connected by a twenty-five gigabit network,</font>

58
00:04:15,367 --> 00:04:18,262
<font color="#FFFFFF">running PyTorch with Horovod and NCCL,</font>

59
00:04:18,342 --> 00:04:25,780
<font color="#FFFFFF">and using memory-momentum error feedback to
preserve accuracy across all sparsification methods.</font>

60
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:32,174
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline result is that DRAGONN cuts
compression time by up to seventy percent compared</font>

61
00:04:32,254 --> 00:04:35,120
<font color="#FFFFFF">to the best existing sparsification methods.</font>

62
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:37,731
<font color="#FFFFFF">Reaching the same level of convergence,</font>

63
00:04:37,811 --> 00:04:44,762
<font color="#FFFFFF">it delivers up to three-and-a-half times higher
total training throughput than Deep Gradient Compression,</font>

64
00:04:44,842 --> 00:04:49,383
<font color="#FFFFFF">and up to nearly thirty-six times over full
gradient synchronization,</font>

65
00:04:49,463 --> 00:04:53,467
<font color="#FFFFFF">all while matching the test accuracy of full
synchronization.</font>

66
00:04:53,547 --> 00:04:57,820
<font color="#FFFFFF">Importantly, its advantage over DGC grows as more
GPUs are added,</font>

67
00:04:57,900 --> 00:05:00,030
<font color="#FFFFFF">which signals strong scalability,</font>

68
00:05:00,110 --> 00:05:04,650
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the authors expect the gains to widen further
on faster networks.</font>

69
00:05:05,030 --> 00:05:09,768
<font color="#FFFFFF">An ablation study separates the contributions of
the three components.</font>

70
00:05:09,848 --> 00:05:14,518
<font color="#FFFFFF">The hashing-based compressor by itself provides the
base improvement;</font>

71
00:05:14,598 --> 00:05:19,199
<font color="#FFFFFF">layering on efficiency-aware tensor selection
increases the speedup,</font>

72
00:05:19,279 --> 00:05:22,985
<font color="#FFFFFF">for example to about one-point-nine times on one model,</font>

73
00:05:23,065 --> 00:05:27,872
<font color="#FFFFFF">and adding sparse decoding pushes it as high as
four-point-three times.</font>

74
00:05:27,952 --> 00:05:32,897
<font color="#FFFFFF">Notably, the benefit of sparse decoding can exceed
the number of workers,</font>

75
00:05:32,977 --> 00:05:39,230
<font color="#FFFFFF">because it eliminates the decode cost that
otherwise grows linearly with the number of GPUs.</font>

76
00:05:39,310 --> 00:05:45,081
<font color="#FFFFFF">Micro-benchmarks confirm DRAGONN has the lowest
encoding time across all tensor sizes</font>

77
00:05:45,161 --> 00:05:49,280
<font color="#FFFFFF">and that its decode time stays nearly flat as GPUs
are added.</font>

78
00:05:49,660 --> 00:05:57,455
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key numbers are: up to a seventy percent
reduction in compression time versus state-of-the-art sparsification;</font>

79
00:05:57,535 --> 00:06:03,395
<font color="#FFFFFF">up to three-and-a-half times faster total training
than DGC and up to thirty-six times</font>

80
00:06:03,475 --> 00:06:07,125
<font color="#FFFFFF">faster than full synchronization at equal convergence;</font>

81
00:06:07,205 --> 00:06:11,546
<font color="#FFFFFF">per-model speedups over DGC of about one-point-four
on ResNet50,</font>

82
00:06:11,626 --> 00:06:15,069
<font color="#FFFFFF">two-point-two on ViT, one-point-seven on MLP-Mixer,</font>

83
00:06:15,149 --> 00:06:18,315
<font color="#FFFFFF">and three-point-five on extreme classification;</font>

84
00:06:18,395 --> 00:06:24,670
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a decoding overhead reduced from growing
linearly with the GPU count to nearly constant.</font>

85
00:06:25,050 --> 00:06:32,604
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting lesson is simple: since gradient
sparsification only ever needs an approximate set of top gradients,</font>

86
00:06:32,684 --> 00:06:36,080
<font color="#FFFFFF">it should not pay for an exact selection algorithm.</font>

87
00:06:36,160 --> 00:06:40,715
<font color="#FFFFFF">By swapping exact parallel prefix sum for direct
randomized hashing,</font>

88
00:06:40,795 --> 00:06:42,556
<font color="#FFFFFF">DRAGONN turns a sequential,</font>

89
00:06:42,636 --> 00:06:47,327
<font color="#FFFFFF">dependency-heavy compression step into an
embarrassingly parallel one,</font>

90
00:06:47,407 --> 00:06:52,848
<font color="#FFFFFF">removes the overhead that had been cancelling out
sparsification's benefits, and,</font>

91
00:06:52,928 --> 00:06:56,324
<font color="#FFFFFF">together with tensor selection and sparse decoding,</font>

92
00:06:56,404 --> 00:07:02,050
<font color="#FFFFFF">makes gradient sparsification finally pay off at
scale without sacrificing accuracy.</font>
